 ### [Edge-Preserving Splatting with Compression/Stretching](6574-edge-preserving-splatting-with-compression-stretching.md)
 Edge-Preserving Splatting is a volume rendering technique that sharpens discontinuities in low-resolution or undersampled data by applying non-linear compression and stretching functions to standard splatting kernels. Unlike conventional splatting, which produces smooth, blurred edges, this method ensures C1 continuity (continuous first derivative) across splat boundaries while enhancing the visual definition of iso-surfaces. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [EWA Splatting](1069-ewa-splatting.md) by explicitly modeling edge geometry (direction and strength) to drive view-dependent footprint selection, rather than relying solely on geometric projection.
 
-### [Noise Splats](8547-noise-splats.md)
-Noise Splats is a multivariate volume rendering technique that extends textured splatting by embedding controlled noise or discrete dots within the reconstruction texture. It solves the problem of representing nonhomogeneous materials (e.g., translucent granite) or synthesizing realistic clouds/fog by using the local amount of noise as a second independent scalar variable. Unlike standard textured splatting which uses anisotropic textures for vector fields, Noise Splats embeds noise directly into the scalar splat footprint, allowing the variance of the rendered image to encode additional data.
-
 ### [Polynomial and Spline Kernels](9995-polynomial-and-spline-kernels.md)
-Polynomial and Spline Kernels replace the standard Gaussian reconstruction kernel in splatting with piecewise polynomial or spline functions to improve rendering accuracy and visual fidelity. They solve the problem of visual artifacts and blurring inherent in Gaussian kernels by providing kernels with compact support, specific decay properties, or tunable shapes that better preserve high-frequency features like edges and small textures. They set themselves apart from siblings like [EWA Splatting](1069-ewa-splatting.md) by focusing on the mathematical form of the kernel function itself rather than just the projection geometry, and from [Splat Extent and Projection](1792-splat-extent-and-projection.md) by defining the internal weight distribution within the footprint.
-
-### [Texture Stretch Weighting](11508-texture-stretch-weighting.md)
-Texture Stretch Weighting (wTS) is a weighting mechanism used in point-based neural rendering to mitigate artifacts caused by weighted average pooling when handling view-dependent effects. It compensates for the distortion of splats in novel views by penalizing pixels at grazing angles, thereby increasing the influence of front-facing views. Unlike standard isotropic weighting, it accounts for the anisotropic stretching of the splat footprint due to perspective projection.
+Polynomial and Spline Kernels refer to a family of splatting methods that replace standard Gaussian reconstruction kernels with piecewise polynomial, spline, or other non-Gaussian functions to control the shape, support, and decay of the splat footprint. They solve the problem of visual artifacts, blurring, or inflexible footprint shapes inherent in Gaussian kernels by providing kernels with compact support, tunable parameters, or specific decay properties that better preserve high-frequency features or enable differentiable optimization. They set themselves apart from siblings like [EWA Splatting](1069-ewa-splatting.md) by focusing on the mathematical form of the kernel function itself rather than just the projection geometry, and from [Splat Extent and Projection](1792-splat-extent-and-projection.md) by defining the internal weight distribution within the footprint.
 
